                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "This is a short and sweet deduction game. None of the cases are terribly difficult from a logic standpoint, though there were some unique deduction methods. The art is really cute as well, and I look forward to playing some generated cases, as the campaign seems to be more or less an introduction to all the deduction methods available.\n\nI have some criticisms of the gameplay, however. \nFirst - the timer. I generally found that I had time to find enough evidence to make my accusation, and I do appreciate the fact that it encourages strategic choices (rather than just asking everyone about everything). However, there were some cases where I ran out of time simply because I had to examine every weapon and ask every suspect about motive - and some suspects refused to answer multiple times. Suspects refusing to answer does not mean that they don't have info - they seem to do so randomly. I think if there is to be a timer, suspects should always answer if they have a response - unless the non-response also takes no time. On that subject, it was a bit frustrating that if I asked someone the same thing twice (to confirm their wording for example), I took a time hit both times. This is not the case in other move-limited games (Blake Manor) that allow revisiting dialogue without a penalty. In general, I don't think the timer is a bad thing if there is a guarantee that a player who plays strategically will find the solution, though I also think many players would appreciate a \"cozy mode\" difficulty setting without a timer.\nRegarding the case design, I felt that the body could have been used to give clues as to the weapon more often than it did. Why would a gunshot or knife victim not at least have blood? This would alleviate the tedium of having to examine every weapon almost every case. Another note - [spoiler]in the painting sequence, one person says they removed the portrait, then the next person says they saw that person leaving and also saw the broken portrait on the floor. How would they see the portrait if the previous person took it? Could it perhaps be that they saw the person leaving ˆwith the portraitˆ? This tripped me up as I thought one of those two had to have been lying.[/spoiler]",
